      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The First Congregational Church, organized in 1822, shared a worship house built in 1829 and rebuilt in 1862, reflecting early religious life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/de_peyster_new_york",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Settlement began November 1802 when Samuel Bristol from Vermont arrived with his family, marking the town’s first settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/de_peyster_new_york",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early settlers expanded rapidly in winter 1803–1804, bringing pioneers from Vermont, Massachusetts, Rhode Island, and Saratoga County via the Old State Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/de_peyster_new_york",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The de Peyster family, wealthy merchants who settled in New Amsterdam in 1657, later became influential politicians, laying the colonial foundation for the town.",
